# M24 — posture rule E1 (the REST exposure is recorded) made unmatchable.
#
# WHY THIS IS THE MUTATION THAT MATTERS MOST IN THIS FILE. The defect rule E1 exists to
# catch was REAL and was shipped: schema `app` was never added to the project's exposed
# schemas, so `app.submit_decision`, `app.append_revision` and `app.stamp_milestone` — the
# whole lifecycle write path, the only legal writer of `submitted_at` (canon 4.6b) — were
# unreachable from the browser. MEASURED against onpkspfxfmjkqpomcsse as
# `PGRST106 Invalid schema: app`, with the control that the same request without
# `Content-Profile` answered a DIFFERENT code, PGRST202.
#
# The whole suite was green while that was true, because every integration test speaks to
# Postgres directly and none crosses PostgREST. So this rule is the only thing in the
# repository that can see the fault, and a rule nothing can prove fires is back to where we
# started.
#
# `if false then` rather than a nonexistent role: M6 recorded that
# `has_function_privilege('__never_a_role__', …)` RAISES, which kills the function and the
# build and so is a mutation that is invalid rather than unmatchable.
id: M24
title: posture rule E1 (pgrst.db_schemas names schema app) made unmatchable
expect: caught
proves: rule E1 reports a missing REST exposure, which is the defect that shipped and that no other test in the suite can observe
expect-test: [NFR-01] E1 fires when pgrst.db_schemas stops naming app
expect-test: [NFR-01] E1 fires when pgrst.db_schemas is absent entirely, and says so

file: supabase/migrations/20261022160000_expose_app_schema_to_postgrest.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
  if not app.pgrst_schema_exposed(recorded, 'app') then
@@REPLACE
  if false then
@@END
